          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you it is \"just age.\" The guidance I have doesn't say that, and I can't diagnose the cause of breathlessness.\n\n**Get help urgently if any of these happen:** breathlessness at rest or when it comes on suddenly; struggling to speak in full sentences; blue or gray lips, tongue, face or fingertips; chest pain together with breathlessness; or feeling drowsy, confused or exhausted while breathing is hard. If you have any of these, call emergency services now. (Source: *Severe difficulty breathing*, emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance.)\n\nYour description (no chest pain, breathless only on hills) did not match the emergency patterns in the material I checked. That does not mean nothing is wrong. The check covers only a fixed list of presentations.\n\nBecause this is new for you, it is reasonable to book a visit with a clinician and mention that you get puffed on hills. They can check your heart and lungs and decide whether anything needs looking into. The guidance I searched did not cover causes of exertional breathlessness, so I can't tell you what might be behind it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
